rem bf1942patch for Battlefield 1942 1.61 (BF1942.exe). Install: copy dinput8.dll, this file and the bf1942patch
rem folder next to BF1942.exe. The log goes to bf1942patch.log next to this file.
rem
rem Every option is a console variable, named next to the game's own: game.* (like game.lockFps), renderer.* (like
rem renderer.fieldOfView), and voiceChat.* for the voice chat. The patch reads this file as it loads, before the game
rem starts. In the game, the console reads an option (game.maxFps) and sets it (game.maxFps 144), and so can any .con
rem file the game runs. Options set in the game (the console, the game's .con files and the options the patch adds to
rem the game's menus) are kept in the player's bf1942patch.con in the save folder (see game.redirectSaves; created on
rem the first start), which wins over this file. Most take effect when the game next starts, which it does for every
rem map; the console says so. Those that apply at once: game.hudScale, game.maxFps, the gamepad's dead zone,
rem sensitivity and keys, the voice keys, volumes, activation and microphone, game.logFiles, game.logRegistry and
rem game.logHud.
rem
rem 1 = on, 0 = off (true / false, on / off and yes / no work too). Text with spaces goes in quotes: "like this".
rem Every option is on by default; set one to 0 to get the original behaviour back.

rem ---------------------------------------------------------------- general
rem 0 = load, but patch nothing
game.patchEnabled 1

rem ---------------------------------------------------------------- saves
rem profiles (options, controls, server lists), the current profile, campaign progress, the server set-up and the
rem screenshots in %USERPROFILE%\Saved Games\DICE\Battlefield 1942 instead of the install folder (for every mod:
rem Mods\<mod>\Settings, Mods\<mod>\save). Existing profiles and saves are copied over the first time a mod is used;
rem files only the install has (the shipped defaults) are still read from there. This and game.saveFolder are only read
rem from this file: they decide where the player's bf1942patch.con is
game.redirectSaves 1
rem use this folder instead (environment variables allowed, e.g. "%APPDATA%\BF1942"); "" = Saved Games
game.saveFolder ""
rem log every file path the patch redirects (1), or every file the game opens (2) (troubleshooting)
game.logFiles 0

rem ---------------------------------------------------------------- registry, CD keys
rem the game never reads or writes the registry: the CD keys come from game.cdKey or a cdkey file, everything else the
rem game keeps there is answered in memory. The game runs from any folder without an installer
game.noRegistry 1
rem log every registry access of the game (troubleshooting)
game.logRegistry 0
rem the CD keys. "" = a file named cdkey (or cdkey.txt) in the save folder or next to BF1942.exe: the key on its first
rem line, and optional lines XPack1=<key> / XPack2=<key> for the expansions (see bf1942patch\cdkey.example). An
rem expansion without a key of its own uses the Battlefield 1942 key
game.cdKey ""
rem The Road to Rome
game.cdKeyXPack1 ""
rem Secret Weapons of WWII
game.cdKeyXPack2 ""
rem the install language the game is told (English, German, French, Spanish, Italian, ...); "" = English
game.installLanguage ""

rem ---------------------------------------------------------------- network
rem the server list and server reporting through OpenSpy (GameSpy is gone): every GameSpy host name goes to its
rem openspy.net twin, and the master server names (master.gamespy.com, master.bf1942.sk, master.bf1942.org) to
rem game.masterServer
game.openSpy 1
rem the master server; "" = master.openspy.net
game.masterServer ""

rem ---------------------------------------------------------------- display
rem a borderless window that covers the monitor instead of exclusive full screen: the desktop resolution never changes
rem (not even when the game restarts itself for every map, see game.seamlessRestart) and alt-tab is instant. Pick
rem the monitor's own resolution in the video options for a sharp picture; any other is scaled to fit
renderer.borderless 1
rem the monitor to use (1, 2, ...; 0 = the main one)
renderer.monitor 0
rem wait for the monitor's refresh (no tearing; the desktop compositor usually prevents it already)
renderer.vSync 0
rem the game sees the monitor's real pixels on a display scaled to more than 100% (no blurry, oversized window)
renderer.dpiAware 1
rem when the profile's video mode is not available (another monitor, another refresh rate) use the same size at any
rem refresh rate, else the desktop's size, else 800x600, instead of stopping with "Invalid videomode specified"
renderer.modeFallback 1
rem with renderer.borderless: the menus (which the game always runs at 800x600) at the monitor's resolution, sharp and
rem at their own shape, instead of a blurry 800x600 picture stretched to the monitor. The game is also told it runs
rem full screen, so it uses the resolution of the video options (game.setGameDisplayMode) instead of 800x600
renderer.nativeMenus 1

rem ---------------------------------------------------------------- frame rate
rem no 60 fps cap (100 with renderer.vSync off): the game runs at up to game.maxFps. game.lockFps <fps> in the console
rem still works, and game.lockFps 0 means no limit. A capped game sleeps while it waits instead of keeping a core busy
game.unlockFps 1
rem frames per second at most with game.unlockFps (30-1000); 0 = no limit (renderer.vSync 1 limits it to the monitor's
rem refresh rate). The game's Options - Video page gets a MAX FPS row for it (0-300)
game.maxFps 0
rem 0 = no MAX FPS row on the Video page (the limit is then only game.maxFps)
game.maxFpsOption 1
rem the HUD and menu timers and fades in real time: the weapon bar, kill and chat messages stay up as long as they do
rem at 60 fps at any frame rate, instead of vanishing early above 60 fps
game.realTimeHud 1

rem ---------------------------------------------------------------- restarts
rem the game starts a new copy of itself for every map, for hosting, joining and going back to the menu. With this the
rem screen keeps showing the loading picture from the old copy to the new one (no desktop, no black flicker, no display
rem mode changes, no BlackScreen.exe) and the new copy gets the keyboard and mouse straight away
game.seamlessRestart 1

rem ---------------------------------------------------------------- widescreen, HUD
rem the 3-D view at the screen's own shape: the vertical view stays as at 4:3 and wide screens see more at the sides
rem (Hor+). Also fixes the culling that cut off terrain patches at the edges of wide screens. Drop old
rem "renderer.fieldOfView 1.33" lines from Video.con: they were a work-around for the stretched picture
renderer.horPlus 1
rem the HUD and the menus at their own shape instead of stretched to the screen (menus and the loading screen stay
rem centred, with black bars beside them)
game.widescreenHud 1
rem while playing, every HUD element sits at the screen edge it belongs to (minimap and kill messages right, chat left,
rem ...) instead of crowding the middle 4:3 part of a wide screen
game.anchorHud 1
rem HUD size in percent (50-200). The game's Options - General page gets a HUD SCALE slider for it, saved with the
rem profile (its GeneralOptions.con runs game.hudScale); this is the value for profiles that have none
game.hudScale 100
rem 0 = no HUD SCALE slider (the HUD scale is then only game.hudScale)
game.hudScaleOption 1

rem ---------------------------------------------------------------- detail
rem how far out things inside the view keep their full detail, in percent of what the game and the map set (100-1000;
rem 100 = as the original). The view distance, the fog and the distance at which objects disappear are never changed:
rem they stay as the map / the server set them, so nobody sees farther than with the original game.
rem meshes (buildings, vehicles, soldiers, props) switch to their coarser versions this much farther away
renderer.meshLod 300
rem trees turn into flat billboards this much farther away (50 m in the original objects)
renderer.treeLod 300
rem the terrain drops to its coarser levels this much farther away (GeometryTemplate.lodDistance of the map)
renderer.terrainLod 300
rem the terrain's triangle budget (GeometryTemplate.targetTriCount of the map, 4000-5000 in the original maps; at most
rem 200000 triangles): finer hills and slopes
renderer.terrainDetail 400

rem ---------------------------------------------------------------- gamepads
rem modern gamepads (Xbox, PlayStation, Switch Pro, ...) through SDL 2, offered to the game as its first joysticks:
rem IDAxis_0/1 left stick, IDAxis_2/3 right stick, IDAxis_4/5 LT/RT; buttons 0 A, 1 B, 2 X, 3 Y, 4 LB, 5 RB, 6 Back,
rem 7 Start, 8/9 stick clicks, 10/11 LT/RT, 12-15 D-pad up/down/left/right
game.gamepad 1
rem stick dead zone, percent (the game has none of its own)
game.gamepadDeadzone 15
rem stick response: 100 = linear, 50 = squared (finer aim near the centre), 0 = cubed
game.gamepadSensitivity 100
rem run Settings\Gamepad.con (in the save folder; written with defaults the first time) after the profile's controls:
rem move / look / fire / jump ... for infantry, land and sea vehicles, aircraft and the menu, next to the keyboard and
rem mouse bindings. The Controls menu would drop these when it saves, so they live in their own file
game.gamepadBindings 1
rem hide a gamepad's own DirectInput entry, so it is not listed twice
game.gamepadHideDuplicates 1
rem keyboard keys (DirectInput key codes) Start / Back also press, e.g. 1 = Escape, 15 = Tab; 0 = none
game.gamepadStartKey 0
game.gamepadBackKey 0

rem ---------------------------------------------------------------- voice chat
rem voice chat with the other players through the LANCommander Interposer (built into dinput8.dll and the ASI; the
rem Interposer plugin build uses the Interposer it runs in), with a game server that runs this patch (it hosts the
rem voice server). The voice server is found from the game server the game joins; players hear the
rem players near them and, on the radio, their own team
voiceChat.enabled 1
rem the voice server's port (UDP), used when the game server does not say which it is
voiceChat.port 27800
rem the voice server's password, if it has one
voiceChat.password ""
rem the voice server's host (then voiceChat.port is used as it is); "" = the game server's
voiceChat.server ""
rem the game server's query ports, asked for its voice port and FastDL server (LAN, internet)
game.queryPorts "22000,23000"
rem 1 = talk to the players nearby whenever the microphone hears speech, without holding a key (voice activation);
rem 0 = push to talk with voiceChat.proximityKey. Also VOICE INPUT MODE in Options - Sound (page 2)
voiceChat.voiceActivation 0
rem the same for the team radio (VOICE INPUT MODE turns it off with PUSH TO TALK)
voiceChat.radioActivation 0
rem the push-to-talk keys, a primary and an alternate one each (either talks), as the game names them (Options -
rem Controls - Common gets rows for both): IDKey_A..IDKey_Z, IDKey_0..IDKey_9, IDKey_F1..IDKey_F12, IDKey_Capital,
rem IDKey_LeftAlt, IDKey_Tab, IDKey_Space, IDKey_Numpad0, ..., Mouse_IDButton_0..Mouse_IDButton_4 (the mouse buttons
rem from the left one); none = no key
voiceChat.proximityKey IDKey_Capital
voiceChat.proximityKeyAlternate none
voiceChat.radioKey Mouse_IDButton_3
voiceChat.radioKeyAlternate none
rem the volume of the other players and the microphone's sensitivity, in percent (0-200). Options - Sound (page 2)
rem gets a slider for each
voiceChat.volume 100
voiceChat.micSensitivity 100
rem the microphone, by its device id (the log names it when it is picked); "" = the system's default. Options - Sound
rem (page 1) gets a MICROPHONE row to pick it, in place of DISABLE ALL SOUND (click to step through them)
voiceChat.inputDevice ""
rem who is talking, under the minimap while playing: you (while your voice is sent) and every player you hear, in their
rem team's chat colour, with a radio icon for the team radio or a megaphone icon for nearby
voiceChat.hud 1
rem 0 = no rows in the Controls and Sound options (the keys, volumes and microphone are then only the options above,
rem and DISABLE ALL SOUND stays)
voiceChat.options 1
rem a MUTE VOICE button on the scoreboard, in place of DROP BUDDY (ADD BUDDY and DROP BUDDY become one button that
rem follows the player picked): pick a player in the list and click it to stop (or start again) hearing them. Mutes
rem are kept by player name with the profile, as the buddies are (Settings\Profiles\<profile>\VoiceMuteList.con; also
rem voiceChat.mute / voiceChat.unmute "<name>" in the console); 0 = no button, and the game's own buddy buttons
rem (the saved mutes still apply)
voiceChat.muteButton 1
rem Linux server only: the voice library when no LANCommander Interposer is preloaded; "" =
rem libLANCommander.Interposer.so next to libbf1942patch.so
voiceChat.library ""

rem ---------------------------------------------------------------- FastDL
rem the maps (and mod archives) the game does not have, downloaded as it joins a server, by the LANCommander
rem Interposer's FastDL (for the one built into dinput8.dll and the ASI, the patch turns FastDL on in
rem .interposer\Config.yml next to it; a LANCommander-deployed Interposer needs FastDL on in its own Config.yml), with a
rem game server that runs this patch and says where its FastDL server is (game.fastDlPort below). The server is asked
rem on its game.queryPorts
game.fastDl 1
rem a FastDL server for game servers that name none (or do not answer), e.g. "http://fastdl.lan:8080/"; "" = none.
rem The game server's own always wins, over this and over the Interposer's FastDL.BaseUrl
game.fastDlUrl ""

rem ---------------------------------------------------------------- dedicated server
rem The dedicated server (BF1942_w32ded.exe, or bf1942_lnxded with libbf1942patch.so) hosts the voice server:
rem voiceChat.enabled, voiceChat.port (the port it listens on, UDP), voiceChat.password and voiceChat.library above
rem apply to it too, and these. The server reads them from this file only (it has no player's file and no console
rem commands for them); on Linux each can also be set with an environment variable BF1942PATCH_<OPTION>, by the
rem option's name in Config.yml (see README.md), e.g. BF1942PATCH_VOICEPORT=27800 for voiceChat.port.
rem the address to listen on; "" = every interface
voiceChat.bindAddress ""
rem enables the voice server's remote admin (LANCommander, comms_admin); "" = none
voiceChat.adminKey ""
rem the most players in voice, and the most bits per second a player's voice is sent with
voiceChat.maxPlayers 256
voiceChat.maxBitrate 32000
rem how far nearby voice carries, in metres: heard at full volume within voiceChat.proximityFullVolumeRange, not at all
rem beyond voiceChat.proximityRange
voiceChat.proximityRange 50
voiceChat.proximityFullVolumeRange 4
rem 1 = the bots are voice players too (testing)
voiceChat.includeBots 0
rem the FastDL server the players download missing maps from, told to them in the query replies (with or without
rem voice chat): an HTTP server with the game's Mods folder at http://<fastDlHost>:<fastDlPort><fastDlPath>Mods/
rem (Mods/<mod>/Archives/bf1942/levels/<map>.rfa). game.fastDlPort 0 = none; game.fastDlHost "" = this server's
rem address; game.fastDlPath "" = /
game.fastDlPort 0
game.fastDlHost ""
game.fastDlPath ""
rem the dedicated server's error dialogs are written to the log and answered OK, so an unattended server exits instead
rem of waiting for a click forever
game.suppressDialogs 1
rem Linux server: 1 = the log (bf1942patch.log next to the server) goes to the console too
game.logToStderr 0

rem ---------------------------------------------------------------- troubleshooting
rem log crashes with registers and a call chain to bf1942patch.log
game.crashLog 0
rem log every HUD box and the screen edge it is anchored to (troubleshooting game.anchorHud)
game.logHud 0
rem log the frame rate every 5 seconds (troubleshooting game.unlockFps)
game.logFps 0
